WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.100
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.767
and comedian you know from
shows such as

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:06.200
"Broad City"
and "The Good Place."

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.300
She currently stars in
the third season of

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.067
the hit HBO series "Barry."

00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:12.667
The season finale airs on
HBO and HBO Max this Sunday.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:13.801
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.701
-[ Whispering ] Sally.

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:18.200
Hey. Sally, wake up.

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.601
It's the whole home screen.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:25.400
[ Gasps, laughs ]

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.133
Look at your freakin' face.
It's huge.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:31.133
That face is being
seen everywhere this morning.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:34.033
And you're still a 98%
on Rotty T's.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.000
-This is insane.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.968
-It's all happening!

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:39.434
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:41.267
our friend D'Arcy Carden,
everybody.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:43.534
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.534
Welcome back, D'Arcy.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:06.701
-[ Laughs ]
-I know.

00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:07.968
And, D'Arcy,
it should be noted, you said,

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:09.634
you know, if Jeff Goldblum
wants to stick around,

00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:10.901
you would be happy with it.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:14.801
-I'm not in charge here,
but if he wants to stay, he can.

00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:16.534
-Well, I think we're all
very happy about that.

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:20.267
Let me just say, I have enjoyed
this season very -- so much.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.968
-Barry much?
-Barry much.

00:01:22.968 --> 00:01:24.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:26.968
But this was a season,
like a lot of great television,

00:01:26.968 --> 00:01:29.133
that was interrupted by COVID.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:31.300
So, how far into
this season were you

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.901
when the rug got pulled out?

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:34.534
-When the thing happened?
-Yeah.

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:37.300
-We were doing table reads, so
we were about to start filming.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.100
We were on the --
You know, L.A. lockdown

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.367
started on a Friday.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.467
We went -- We did our table read
on a Wednesday,

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:45.701
when it was still like,
"What's going on?"

00:01:45.701 --> 00:01:46.801
-Yeah.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:48.133
-And we went to our
little table read,

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.801
and everyone had like
one individual hand sanitizer,

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:52.634
like that would fix it.

00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:53.767
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:55.033
And everybody was like,
"Should we hug?

00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:56.534
Should we not?"

00:01:56.534 --> 00:02:00.467
And then a couple of actors and
I got into Henry Winkler's car

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.534
and drove to lunch and,
like, shared food, appetizers.

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:04.734
So we didn't know anything
at that point.

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:06.033
We knew absolutely nothing.

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:10.033
And then we got the call
that the world did the thing.

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:12.467
-Even after lockdown,
if Henry Winkler asked me

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:13.501
to get in his car, I would.

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:14.534
For what it's worth.
-You have to.

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.601
Yeah, you have to.

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.501
-I wouldn't say no to
Henry Winkler.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:21.167
-You know, like every morning,
I called Jeff and said,

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:23.000
"What are we going to
wear today?"

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:25.968
-It is...

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:27.467
And let me just stress,
you might be

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:30.033
the first two people
who have ever been on this show

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:31.801
that went
black, yellow, and green.

00:02:31.801 --> 00:02:34.267
-That's not a normal combo?

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:35.501
-And here you are together.

00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:37.501
-I said, "I'll wear my pj's."

00:02:37.501 --> 00:02:39.000
You wear a cool cardigan.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.133
And our son will just wear
a nice blue shirt."

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:45.801
-Mom, dad, why do you guys
have to dress alike?!

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:48.067
-We laid out your clothes.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:52.200
You refused to wear your
black, yellow, and green.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.801
-I know. I'm so sorry.

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:57.501
This is -- I want to say
how really deeply excited I am.

00:02:57.501 --> 00:02:59.801
A trailer just
was out this week.

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:01.567
You are in
a television adaptation of

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:02.634
"A League of Their Own."

00:03:02.634 --> 00:03:03.801
-I am.

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:06.767
-A perfect film that
I rewatched recently,

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:07.968
and I'm wondering --

00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:09.167
Obviously,
I'm a little bit older than you.

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:10.501
Did that movie
mean something to you?

00:03:10.501 --> 00:03:12.734
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
It totally did.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:14.367
You know,
I played baseball as a kid

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:16.067
and I played a bunch of sports,
and that one --

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:19.734
There was, like, so many good
'90s baseball movies,

00:03:19.734 --> 00:03:22.234
but this one
really, really was --

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:23.267
meant something special to me.

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:24.701
Uh-oh. Oh, yeah.
-Well, you have a photo.

00:03:24.701 --> 00:03:28.200
You mentioned you played
baseball, so I want to, um...

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.067
So, this is --
Is this a photo of you

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:31.234
that was then made into
a button?

00:03:31.234 --> 00:03:33.634
-You got that right.
-Yeah, okay.

00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:36.300
-Really just, like,
I want to just point out

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.601
I did that myself.

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:38.834
-Yeah.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:40.601
It's really nice.
-I was a little kid.

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:45.133
I wasn't, like, a hairstylist,
but I was able to create that.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:46.801
Like, that's...
-Yeah.

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:48.067
And, now, are you still --
-That's not nothing.

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:52.033
You're still doing work with
BDT &amp; Associates, right?

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:53.667
-Yes, yes.

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:54.734
-This is wonderful.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.100
Also, I feel like
a lot of people did --

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.467
in this era, did a lot of sports
with turtlenecks, right?

00:03:58.467 --> 00:03:59.934
-Yeah. Or, like,
a nice mock turtleneck.

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:01.434
-Just a mock turtleneck, yeah.

00:04:01.434 --> 00:04:02.434
-Can I just tell you that --
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:03.434
I'm sorry, Jeff.

00:04:03.434 --> 00:04:04.834
I didn't mean to
shout out your...

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:06.868
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.701
-Another '90s movie that meant
the absolute world to me

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:10.801
was "Jurassic Park."

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.467
Is "Jurassic Park."
-Yeah.

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:14.601
-I'm a huge --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:14.601 --> 00:04:16.000
-It is nice, right?
-As we all are.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.167
-Yeah.
-Nice segue?

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:18.434
-Yeah, very nice.
-Thanks.

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:19.701
-You could do this.
-Okay, great. Let's switch.

00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:22.067
-You could do this.
Very natural segue.

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:24.200
-I have to tell you,
my parents took my --

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.968
I almost just said "children" --

00:04:25.968 --> 00:04:28.701
my siblings and I out of school
to see opening day of

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:29.767
"Jurassic Park."

00:04:29.767 --> 00:04:32.734
And I was about that age,
maybe --

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:35.000
Yeah, about that age,
a little bit --

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.300
-So older than this.

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:40.567
Somewhere between
these two ages?

00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:41.567
Yeah, okay. Gotcha.

00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:45.067
-I really like that part.
So cute.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.767
And then let's just look
at the nose.

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:48.734
-Oh, yeah. That's great.
That's a beautiful photo.

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:49.834
-Noses change, though.

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:52.334
-They do, yeah.
Can we get a close-up?

00:04:52.334 --> 00:04:53.968
-Noses change.

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:56.634
Noses change.
They don't tell you that.

00:04:56.634 --> 00:04:57.901
-They don't tell you that.

00:04:57.901 --> 00:04:59.901
So, you got taken out of school.
-Yes.

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:02.968
And we were watching the movie,
and there was a moment

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:06.033
during the movie when you guys
see the first dinosaur,

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:07.901
where, again, I was too old
to have this thought,

00:05:07.901 --> 00:05:12.000
but I was I was like,
"Oh, dinosaurs do exist?

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.968
Oh, they always say they don't,
but I guess they do.

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:16.300
Somewhere, they do."

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.367
-The funny thing about that is,
that's exactly what

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:22.734
teachers say why not to
take your kids out of school.

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:26.133
Like, you not only did you
miss school,

00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:27.501
you came back with,
like, bad info.

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:28.801
-Totally.
-Yeah.

00:05:28.801 --> 00:05:31.100
-I didn't learn about
any planets that day.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.634
-But I did not know this
about your parents.

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:37.200
They were super-cool
Bay-area music --

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.334
More than fans.

00:05:38.334 --> 00:05:39.901
They had a music magazine.
-They did, yeah.

00:05:39.901 --> 00:05:41.300
My parents had a music magazine
called "BAM."

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.834
-BAM.
-What the hell?

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:46.834
-I have every back copy of it.
-Jeff, are you kidding?

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:48.000
-Yes.
-Okay, you are.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.267
But you -- Did you --
Do you remember BAM?

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:51.601
No, it's okay if you don't.
Let's not do this.

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:53.901
-No, I kind of do.
Yes, yes, yes.

00:05:53.901 --> 00:05:56.267
-It was like in
the '70s, '80s, and '90s.

00:05:56.267 --> 00:05:57.434
-Oh, wow!
-Yeah.

00:05:57.434 --> 00:06:00.300
-So it had a run.
-Yeah, it had a really good run.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.834
And, yeah, it was
really a cool way to grow up,

00:06:02.834 --> 00:06:04.334
because we got to go to concerts

00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:07.501
and we would get
boxes of tapes and CDs

00:06:07.501 --> 00:06:08.868
and we would --

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.868
-And did your parents have
an expectation that, as a child,

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:13.534
you were supposed to be
cooler than your peers

00:06:13.534 --> 00:06:14.634
as far as your music knowledge?

00:06:14.634 --> 00:06:16.601
-They were kind of --
Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:18.534
-Okay.
-Yeah. My mom especially.

00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:20.834
It was my dad's magazine,
but my mom was like...

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:24.167
She was -- She'd be driving
in her little minivan,

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:26.200
and whoever was playing,
she would be like,

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:27.767
"Who's playing drums on this?

00:06:27.767 --> 00:06:28.901
Who produced this?

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:30.200
Who's doing backup vocals
on this?"

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.701
And we would scramble.

00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:33.601
Whoever could answer first was,
like, mom's favorite that day.

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:35.000
-Wow!

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.767
And this is, like, '70s and
'80s, like, rock 'n' roll music?

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:40.167
-Yes. Totally. Yeah, yeah.

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:42.267
Whatever -- It was old stuff,
but it was like, you know,

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:43.634
whatever was new
in California at the time

00:06:43.634 --> 00:06:44.901
we were listening to.

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:47.834
-And you did as a --
You got to meet Huey Lewis.

00:06:47.834 --> 00:06:51.767
-Yeah. A few times.
Sorry to brag.

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:53.701
-That's a huge deal.
I would be very excited by that.

00:06:53.701 --> 00:06:54.834
-I've never met Huey Lewis.
-Have you met Huey Lewis?

00:06:54.834 --> 00:06:56.033
-I've never met --

00:06:56.033 --> 00:06:57.367
-Okay, so you're telling me
I'm the only --

00:06:57.367 --> 00:06:59.067
Have you guys met Huey Lewis?
You have. Okay, cool.

00:06:59.067 --> 00:07:00.701
-Alright, great.
-Yeah.

00:07:00.701 --> 00:07:04.300
That guy frickin' rocks. Yeah.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.534
-But how did --
Tell us the story of how --

00:07:06.534 --> 00:07:09.434
Because -- I feel like you also
met him in a unique way.

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:11.501
It's, one, exciting to meet him,
but, two, the way you met him.

00:07:11.501 --> 00:07:13.000
-I think I was about
5 years old,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.000
again, a little younger than --
maybe about the age of tummy.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.667
-Okay.
-Tummy and nose, yeah.

00:07:18.667 --> 00:07:20.434
We're thinking this age.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:07:20.434 --> 00:07:22.467
-My parents took
my older sister, Laney, and I

00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:26.234
to see Huey Lewis and the News.

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:30.334
And my dad was buds with him,
so after the concert --

00:07:30.334 --> 00:07:31.834
It was my first concert.

00:07:31.834 --> 00:07:34.300
And after the concert, we went
backstage, and everybody, like,

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:35.701
sort of couldn't find Huey.

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:37.634
And, apparently, he had had
what he thought was

00:07:37.634 --> 00:07:39.234
a bad concert, a bad show.

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:41.367
-Impossible.
-Impossible. Thank you.

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.567
And then my parents sort of,
like, lost sight of me,

00:07:43.567 --> 00:07:44.968
little 5-year-old D'Arcy.

00:07:44.968 --> 00:07:47.334
And I come around the corner,
like, holding Huey's hand.

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.567
And, guys, he had
a huge smile on his face.

00:07:49.567 --> 00:07:50.901
I think I cheered him up.

00:07:50.901 --> 00:07:52.968
-Yeah, a little -- I think if
a 5-year-old comes --

00:07:52.968 --> 00:07:55.701
I will say, that is kind of
what I think about, like,

00:07:55.701 --> 00:07:56.767
parents who have
a music magazine.

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:57.801
They're like,
"Oh, where's our kid?"

00:07:57.801 --> 00:07:58.834
-Yeah, yeah, totally.

00:07:58.834 --> 00:08:01.567
I'm like, "Also,
keep an eye on your kid.

00:08:01.567 --> 00:08:04.767
Keep an eye -- Maybe don't take
your kids out of school and

00:08:04.767 --> 00:08:05.934
keep an eye on your kid when
you're backstage at a concert."

00:08:05.934 --> 00:08:07.801
-That was also a
middle-of-the-day Huey Lewis

00:08:07.801 --> 00:08:09.100
concert that they'd taken
you out of school for.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.434
-Yeah, yeah. They took me out --
Yeah, it was the SATs.

00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:11.868
-The teachers were like,
"You know, they do, like --

00:08:11.868 --> 00:08:13.000
They show movies at night,
as well.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.267
You could, like..."

00:08:14.267 --> 00:08:15.434
-"You have a whole weekend."
-"You could do both.

00:08:15.434 --> 00:08:16.901
You could do school and go see
'Jurassic Park.'

00:08:16.901 --> 00:08:18.234
-I think we needed to see
the first showing.

00:08:18.234 --> 00:08:19.567
That was the whole thing.

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.734
-And, so, at what time in your
life -- And, again --

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.400
Well, I actually want to go back
to "A League of Their Own"

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:24.534
to finish it.

00:08:24.534 --> 00:08:27.367
You obviously had to play
a lot of baseball.

00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:29.801
-Yeah.
-Did the skills come back?

00:08:29.801 --> 00:08:31.667
-Oh, honey, they never left.

00:08:31.667 --> 00:08:33.501
-Oh!
[ Laughter ]

00:08:33.501 --> 00:08:34.934
-I don't mean to be like that.

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:36.367
I don't mean to be like that.
-That's great!

00:08:36.367 --> 00:08:37.834
-Do you play baseball?
-Do I ever, yes.

00:08:37.834 --> 00:08:40.100
-You know what? You know what?

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.267
-Are you showing us
how you play baseball?

00:08:41.267 --> 00:08:43.200
-I'm just starting to play.
I like to pitch.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.834
-You know what?
You know what's funny?

00:08:44.834 --> 00:08:46.200
We got three
Pittsburgh guys up here.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.501
-We do. This is all Pittsburgh,

00:08:47.501 --> 00:08:48.801
'cause you shot "League of Their
Own" in Pittsburgh.

00:08:48.801 --> 00:08:50.300
-We shot "League of Their Own"
in Pittsburgh.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:51.567
-And you fell in love with it.
You fell in love with the city.

00:08:51.567 --> 00:08:52.734
-I totally did.
-All you got to do is go there.

00:08:52.734 --> 00:08:53.868
-Yeah, that's true.

00:08:53.868 --> 00:08:55.801
And that's --
For the audience at home,

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:57.067
all you gotta do is go there.

00:08:57.067 --> 00:08:58.300
You'll fall in love with it.
Were you born there?

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.334
-No, my dad was, yeah.

00:08:59.334 --> 00:09:00.567
-Okay, daddy was born there,

00:09:00.567 --> 00:09:02.067
but then you lived there
when you were a baby.

00:09:02.067 --> 00:09:03.701
When you were
just about that age.

